@@1
From: Susan Oyelaran
To: All
Subj: Hello, world, the ARM way
Date: 07-12-88 21:10

Since this is Programmers' Corner and nobody has posted any code yet,
here's the smallest useful thing: printing a string through DOS, from C
with inline ARM assembler (works in the C compilers for ARM-DOS).

On an ARM PC, "INT 21h" is the instruction  svc #0x21.  The registers
map straight across: AX is r0, BX r1, CX r2, DX r3.  A pointer that
would be DS:DX on a PC is just a flat 32-bit address in r3.

  static void dos_print(const char *s)
  {
      __asm__ volatile (
          "mov r3, %0\n\t"          /* DS:DX -> the string  */
          "mov r0, #0x0900\n\t"     /* AH = 09h             */
          "svc #0x21"               /* INT 21h              */
          : : "r" (s) : "r0", "r1", "r2", "r3", "memory");
  }

  dos_print("Hello from svc #0x21!\r\n$");

Yes, the string ends in a dollar sign.  Function 09h prints until it
sees one.  Don't ask me why.  It was like that when I got here.

Susan
