From: Europa Sysop
To: All
Subj: Writing straight to the screen
Date: 08-20-88 23:40

The BBS's sysop screen doesn't go through DOS at all; it writes to the
video memory directly.  In text mode the screen is 80 x 25 cells at
address 0xB8000, each cell a 16-bit value: the character in the low
byte, the colour attribute in the high byte.

  static void putcell(int x, int y, int ch, int attr)
  {
      unsigned short *vram = (unsigned short *)0xB8000;
      vram[y * 80 + x] = (unsigned short)((attr << 8) | ch);
  }

  putcell(79, 0, 'A', 0x1F);    /* white A on blue, top right */

The attribute is background * 16 + foreground: 0x1F is bright white
(15) on blue (1), 0x4E is yellow on red, 0x07 is plain grey on black.
Add 0x80 and it blinks, which you should use exactly once in your life.

On a PC you'd need a far pointer and the segment B800h.  Here it's a
pointer.  I still smile every time.

-- Europa
