From: Karen Whitfield
To: All
Subj: How fast is my machine?  Ask it.
Date: 09-30-88 07:15

Every ARM/AT has a little system-board port that answers questions
about itself.  I/O ports live at 0x10000000 + the port number, so you
read them with a plain load:

  #define PORT(p) (*(volatile unsigned char *)(0x10000000 + (p)))

  printf("MHZ=%u TURBO=%u ID=%c\n", PORT(0xF1), PORT(0xF2), PORT(0xF0));

Port F1h is the clock in MHz (the same number as on the front panel),
F2h is 1 when TURBO is on, and F0h reads the letter 'A', for ARM.  With
TURBO on, mine prints

  MHZ=100 TURBO=1 ID=A

And in assembler, the same port read is two instructions:

      ldr   r1, =0x100000F1
      ldrb  r0, [r1]            @ r0 = MHz

The volatile matters: without it the compiler may decide it already
knows the answer and never ask the hardware again.

KW
