From: Susan Oyelaran
To: All
Subj: Branchless code: conditional execution
Date: 01-21-89 22:40

My favourite thing about the ARM: EVERY instruction can be conditional,
not just jumps.  Four condition bits on top of every instruction.  So
small decisions don't need a branch at all:

  absolute value of r0:
      cmp   r0, #0
      rsblt r0, r0, #0          @ if negative, r0 = 0 - r0

  clamp r0 to 0..255 (colour values, sound samples):
      cmp   r0, #0
      movlt r0, #0
      cmp   r0, #255
      movgt r0, #255

No jumps, so nothing to mispredict and no pipeline refill.  From C:

  static int clamp255(int x)
  {
      __asm__ ("cmp %0, #0\n\t"   "movlt %0, #0\n\t"
               "cmp %0, #255\n\t" "movgt %0, #255"
               : "+r" (x) : : "cc");
      return x;
  }

clamp255(-3) is 0, clamp255(100) is 100, clamp255(999) is 255.  The
compiler does this by itself a lot of the time, but it's nice to know
why the code it makes is so short.

Susan
... ARM-DOS: because RISC is its own reward.
