From: Susan Oyelaran
To: All
Subj: CLZ: log2 in one instruction
Date: 03-08-89 21:55

ARMv5 added CLZ, Count Leading Zeros: how many zero bits come before
the first 1, from the top.  CLZ of 1 is 31, CLZ of 0x80000000 is 0,
CLZ of 0 is 32.  Which means the integer log2 of a number is free:

  static int log2i(unsigned x)
  {
      unsigned n;
      __asm__ ("clz %0, %1" : "=r" (n) : "r" (x));
      return 31 - (int)n;
  }

log2i(1) = 0, log2i(1000) = 9, log2i(0x80000000) = 31.

Uses: "how many bits do I need", rounding up to a power of two for a
memory allocator, normalising numbers in software floating point, and
impressing people at Sal's Pizza.

Susan
