From: Doc Mantissa
To: Susan Oyelaran
Subj: Re: CLZ: log2 in one instruction
Date: 03-09-89 23:30

> and impressing people at Sal's Pizza.

It worked on me.  In exchange, a gift from The Floating Point
(555-7734): 16.16 fixed-point multiply for fractals, without floats.
A number is stored times 65536.  Multiply two of them with SMULL,
which gives the full 64-bit product in two registers, and take the
middle 32 bits:

  static int fixmul(int a, int b)       /* (a * b) >> 16 */
  {
      int r;
      __asm__ ("smull r2, r3, %1, %2\n\t"
               "mov   %0, r2, lsr #16\n\t"
               "orr   %0, %0, r3, lsl #16"
               : "=&r" (r) : "r" (a), "r" (b) : "r2", "r3");
      return r;
  }

3.5 is 229376, -2.25 is -147456.  fixmul(229376, -147456) = -516096,
which is -7.875 times 65536.  Correct, and not a float in sight.

(UMULL is the unsigned twin.  0x87654321 * 1000 has a high word of 528,
if you want to check your work.)

My Mandelbrot got four times faster.  I'm told I shouted.

Doc Mantissa
... There are 10 kinds of people.  I'm the one with the fractal.
