From: Susan Oyelaran
To: All
Subj: TCC -run: C with no waiting
Date: 02-20-90 22:10

For quick experiments I've stopped building EXE files at all.  The
little C compiler that lives in C:\DOS (TCC) can compile a program into
memory and run it straight away:

  TCC -run HELLO.C

No EXE left behind, no link step to wait for, and it takes the inline
ARM assembler in all the snippets in this area.  Look in C:\TC\SAMPLES
for ASMDEMO.C, which reads the CPU's ID register, uses CLZ and UMULL,
and calls svc #0x21, all in one page.

When the experiment works, TCC HELLO.C makes a real HELLO.EXE.

Susan
... Compile, run, repeat.  Coffee optional.
@@19
From: Marcus Feld
To: All
Subj: Making the speaker beep
Date: 03-15-90 19:25

Figured this out from a PC book and it works on the ARM too, because
the ports are the same, they just live at 0x10000000 + port.  The
speaker is driven by timer channel 2:

  #define PORT(p) (*(volatile unsigned char *)(0x10000000 + (p)))

  static void beep(unsigned hz)
  {
      unsigned div = 1193182 / hz;  /* the timer runs at 1.193182 MHz */
      PORT(0x43) = 0xB6;            /* channel 2, square wave */
      PORT(0x42) = div & 0xFF;
      PORT(0x42) = div >> 8;
      PORT(0x61) |= 3;              /* gate on, speaker on */
  }
  static void quiet(void) { PORT(0x61) &= ~3; }

beep(880), wait a few ticks (see Bill's message about 0x46C), quiet().
My program now plays the first four notes of a song I can't name
because my mother is reading over my shoulder.
